<h1>Checksum Mismatch: What It Means and How to Fix It</h1>
<p>A checksum mismatch means the hash you calculated does not match the expected hash. That sounds alarming, but the cause is not always malicious. It may be a failed download, wrong file version, copy error, text encoding issue, line-ending change, or a simple mistake when copying the expected checksum. The right response is to slow down and verify the basics.</p>
<p>Start by confirming the algorithm. An MD5 value will not match a SHA-256 value. MD5 values are commonly shown as 32 hexadecimal characters, while SHA-256 values are usually 64 hexadecimal characters. If the publisher says SHA256, do not run MD5 locally. If the old documentation says MD5, do not compare it with SHA-256 output.</p>
<p>Next, confirm the file. This is the most common problem. You may have checked a compressed archive instead of the extracted file, a shortcut instead of the real file, or version 2.1 instead of version 2.1.1. Browser partial downloads can also leave temporary files. Hash the final file after the download is complete.</p>
<p>Then check the source of the expected hash. Use the official release page, vendor documentation, signed manifest, or trusted repository. Do not copy hashes from random mirrors unless the project tells you to. A checksum only helps when the expected value comes from a source you trust.</p>
<p>On Windows, Microsoft documents certutil with the -hashfile option for generating file hashes. On Linux, GNU coreutils includes checksum tools. On macOS, OpenSSL's dgst command can calculate message digests. If you are unsure about commands, return to <a href="/verify-file-checksums-windows-mac-linux.html">How to Verify File Checksums</a> and repeat the process carefully.</p>
<p>For text files, watch line endings. A file saved with Windows CRLF line endings may hash differently from a file saved with Unix LF line endings, even if the visible text looks the same. Character encoding matters too. UTF-8 and another encoding can produce different bytes, which means different hashes.</p>
<p>If the mismatch remains after checking these basics, download the file again from the official source. If it still fails, look for release notes or known issues. Sometimes maintainers update a file but forget to update the checksum on the page. That is a publishing error, but you should not ignore it. Report it or wait for confirmation.</p>
<p>Do not edit a file to make a checksum match. That sounds obvious, but people sometimes normalize text files, rezip archives, or rename packages while troubleshooting. Those actions create a new file with a new hash. Verification should compare the delivered file against the expected value, not reshape the file until the numbers look right.</p>
<p>If you work in a team, capture the mismatch details before retrying. Save the calculated hash, expected hash, file name, file size, source URL, and time of download. That information helps support teams identify whether the issue is user error, a bad mirror, or a release page mistake.</p>
<p>The final rule is conservative: never install or trust an important file when the checksum does not match and you cannot explain why. A mismatch is a signal to investigate. Most causes are ordinary, but the habit of stopping protects you from the cases that are not.</p>
